<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Marino</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of San Marino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Marino, officially the Republic of San Marino, is a landlocked country in Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, completely surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It claims to have been founded in 301 CE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-marinus">Saint Marinus</a>, a stonemason said to have fled persecution under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and built a chapel on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-titano">Monte Titano</a>, and to be the oldest extant sovereign state. Its borders have been unchanged since 1463. Its independence survived occupation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> in 1503 and by a papal legate in 1739, and in the 19th century it sheltered supporters of Italian unification, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>. It remained neutral in both world wars, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> in 1988 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-marino.jpg" alt="San Marino" width="330" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_San_Marino.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>San Marino is an enclave surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the north-eastern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennine Mountains</a> and about ten kilometres from the Adriatic coast near Rimini. Its land area is just over 61 square kilometres, which makes it the fifth-smallest country in the world and the larger of the two microstates of the Italian Peninsula, the other being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a>. The terrain is hilly with little naturally flat ground. The summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-titano">Monte Titano</a>, on which the capital stands, reaches 749 metres, and the lowest point is the Ausa River at 55 metres. The largest settlement is Dogana, in the municipality of Serravalle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to accounts first recorded centuries after he is suggested to have lived, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-marinus">Saint Marinus</a> left the island of Rab with his friend Leo and went to Rimini as a stonemason. After the Diocletianic Persecution that followed his Christian sermons he escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-titano">Monte Titano</a>, where he built a small church. William Miller described these accounts as a mixture of fables and miracles that perhaps contain some grains of fact. The earliest historical evidence for a monastic community there dates to the 5th or 6th century, when a monk named Eugippus recorded that another monk had lived in a monastery in the area.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> on 24 May 1915, San Marino stayed neutral. Italy suspected that the republic's new radiotelegraph station might give Austrian spies access, tried to establish a detachment of Carabinieri there and cut its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> lines when it refused. Two groups of ten volunteers nevertheless joined the Italian front, the second as a medical corps running a Red Cross field hospital, and the existence of that hospital led Austria-Hungary to suspend diplomatic relations. San Marino and Italy have been bound by conventions since 1862, and the Statutes of 1600 remain at the core of the constitutional framework.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is the City of San Marino, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-titano">Monte Titano</a>, and the country is divided into nine castelli. The Grand and General Council, an elected legislature, selects two heads of state, the Captains Regent, every six months from opposing political parties; they serve together with equal powers. The official language is Italian, and Sammarinese, a dialect of Romagnol, is spoken mainly by the elderly. The euro is the currency, although San Marino is not in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. National defence is by arrangement the responsibility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Crossbow Corps, about 80 volunteers who have given crossbow demonstrations at festivals since 1295, is now purely ceremonial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “San Marino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Andorra also features in debates about microstates: its admission to the United Nations in 1993, in the same years as <b>San Marino</b> and Monaco, helped show that very small states could take full part in international organisations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It is often described as the only member of the old Imperial Diet’s Council of Princes that survives as a sovereign state, and with Monaco, <b>San Marino</b> and Andorra it is one of the European microstates that outlived the consolidation of larger nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>San Marino</b>, by tradition founded in 301, calls itself the world’s oldest surviving republic; its heads of state, two Captains Regent who rule jointly, still serve for only six months at a time.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
